      "id": "alloy_system_1",
      "name": "Mo-Si-B alloy",
      "material_category": "refractory metal intermetallic alloy",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 1,
          "section": "Abstract",
          "quote": "Mo-Si-B alloys can offer higher temperature capability than Ni-base superalloys with proper balancing of the creep, ductility, and oxidation resistance through microstructure optimization.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "e76f3db76354d4e3:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": 13,
          "section": "Model Validation",
          "quote": "A relatively recent study on the fabrication, strength, and oxidation of Mo-Si-B alloys includes high-temperature tensile tests of a three-phase Mo-Si-B alloy including the α-Mo, A15, and T2 phases [23].",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "e76f3db76354d4e3:part:002"
        },
        {
          "page": 13,
          "section": "Summary and Conclusions",
          "quote": "A parametric study of three-phase Mo-Si-B alloys containing the α-Mo, A15, and T2 phases is presented to capture the microstructure-sensitive modulus, yield strength, fatigue resistance, and susceptibility to microcracking related to relative ductility.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "e76f3db76354d4e3:part:002"
        },
        {
          "page": 15,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "this work is the development of an efficient computational framework to explore the material design space of Mo-Si-B alloys for multi-property optimization.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "e76f3db76354d4e3:part:003"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
